From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:30:00 +0000
Subject: media: cedrus: VP9: resize the CAPTURE queue while streaming

A VP9 inter frame may be larger than the frames before it and still
predict from them through the scaled motion compensation
(vp90-2-22-svc_1280x720_3 grows from 320x180 to 640x360 and 1280x720 on
inter frames, with one key frame in the whole stream). The in-place
decoding of smaller frames does not cover this: a larger frame needs
larger CAPTURE buffers, while the references of the old size have to
stay in theirs. The driver rejects such a frame with -EINVAL, and a
client cannot do better, because the only way to get larger buffers is
to stream off and reallocate the CAPTURE queue, which frees the
references.

Support the VIDIOC_REMOVE_BUFS sequence instead, as the verisilicon
driver and GStreamer's v4l2codecs do: while streaming, userspace sets
the new coded size on the OUTPUT queue (same coded format) and the
CAPTURE format follows, creates CAPTURE buffers of the new format with
VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS and removes the old ones with VIDIOC_REMOVE_BUFS
once they are no longer referenced. For that

- the VIDIOC_REMOVE_BUFS ioctl is wired up (v4l2_m2m_ioctl_remove_bufs),
  which makes REQBUFS and CREATE_BUFS report
  V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_REMOVE_BUFS;
- S_FMT(OUTPUT) with the same coded format and S_FMT(CAPTURE) with the
  same pixel format are accepted while the queues have buffers, for VP9
  only: the other decoders take the layout of their references from the
  current CAPTURE format and keep the rule that a format change needs
  empty queues;
- every CAPTURE buffer records the CAPTURE format it was prepared with
  (struct cedrus_buffer.fmt), and the VP9 decoder reads the current
  frame and each reference in the layout of its own buffer. Frames
  queued before the change are decoded in the layout userspace
  prepared them with, references of the previous size at their own
  plane offsets; the hardware already derives the line stride of each
  reference from its width;
- the CAPTURE queue allows 64 buffers: the sets of buffers of the old
  and the new size overlap, and the svc vector grows twice within three
  frames, so three sets of GStreamer's 14 buffers exceed the default
  of 32.

A frame larger than its own buffer is still rejected with -EINVAL;
cedrus_dst_p010_output_set() takes the layout of the buffer and of the
visible picture separately (the same format for H.265). The
10-bit low-2-bit plane offset register is programmed for the current
frame only; whether the hardware derives it for references of another
size as it does the stride is unknown (no 10-bit resize vector exists).

GStreamer 1.28 takes this path on its own when the driver reports
V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_REMOVE_BUFS (an inter frame that does not fit in
the buffers renegotiates without streaming off); smaller inter frames
keep being decoded in place.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
